(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).afoqt=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt.full||[]).push({
  num: 5, part: `Part Two: The Truth`, title: `Rate Problems`, weeks: `March 8 to 20, 2027`,
  tasks: [`AR`,`SDI`,`SJ`,`PS`,`TR`,`WK`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `She did Vern's twenty five word problems in twenty seven of the twenty nine minutes, and he marked them with his pencil while she sat across from him with her coat still on. Nineteen right. He wrote the number at the top of the first page and went through the six she'd missed one at a time, slow, as if nothing else had been said in that room all afternoon. Two were rates where she'd mixed up minutes and hours. He circled those twice.`,
    `At five he locked the heritage room. Out in the lot he said Tuesday, and she said Tuesday.`,
    `Then she drove back across the ramp to the shop, which was dark except for the light over the coffee, and opened her toolbox. She lifted the shadow board foam out by one corner, the way you'd lift a floor mat looking for a spare key. The papers were where they'd been for seven years, soft at the creases. She took the top one and put the foam back down over the rest. Then she buttoned the sheet into the inside pocket of her coat and drove to the plant for an eleven o'clock shift.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Vern's Nav Table: rates, and describing yourself`,
    sections: [
      { h: `One sentence under every rate problem`,
        body: [`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Thirty one years in dispatch, and nearly every number I signed was a rate times a time. Fuel, a front moving east, a crew's duty day. The airplanes changed every few years. That sentence never did."</span>`,
          `Arithmetic Reasoning is twenty five word problems in twenty nine minutes, five choices apiece, with scratch paper. I couldn't tell you for certain whether a calculator's allowed, so practice like it isn't. That works out to a little over a minute a problem, and most of your minute goes to reading the thing and setting it up. The arithmetic's usually the short part.`,
          `A lot of them are rate problems in a different shirt. Underneath every one is the same sentence, amount equals rate times time. Fuel burned is burn rate times hours. Miles is speed times time. Gallons is gallons a minute times minutes. Got two of the three, you've got the third: amount over rate gives you the time, amount over time gives you the rate.`,
          `I wrote the units next to every number on my nav log for twenty years, and I'd tell you to do the same on scratch paper. Pounds over pounds a minute leaves you minutes. If what's left over doesn't match what they asked for, you set it up wrong, and you found out for free.`],
        terms: [[`Rate`,`An amount per unit of something: pounds a minute, miles an hour, drops a minute.`]],
        exam: `Before you calculate, name the three pieces in your head. Whichever one they're asking for is the one you don't have.` },
      { h: `Per what?`,
        body: [`Two rates only compare when they're per the same thing. Twenty one drops in three minutes and twenty four in six sound close. Per minute they're seven and four, and the first is three quarters faster. Put both on the same clock before you decide anything.`,
          `Clocks are where most of the misses live. An hour isn't a hundred minutes. Three hours and forty minutes isn't 3.4 hours. It's three and two thirds. Turn hours and minutes into minutes, or into a fraction of an hour you can work with: fifteen minutes is a quarter, twenty is a third, forty five is three quarters.`,
          `Watch which way a rate points. Gallons a day and days per gallon are upside down from each other. Before you divide, ask which one the question is holding.`],
        terms: [[`Unit rate`,`A rate per one of something: per minute, per hour, per gallon. The fair way to compare two rates.`]],
        exam: `When a time is in hours and minutes, convert it before you multiply. Five hours thirty six minutes is 5.6 hours, not 5.36.` },
      { h: `Rates that change partway`,
        body: [`Some problems change the rate in the middle. A truck runs one stretch at one speed and the rest at another. A tank fills fast until it's nearly full and then slow. Cut the problem where the rate changes, work each piece on its own, and add the pieces.`,
          `Don't average the two rates unless each one ran for the same length of time. Forty five miles an hour and sixty average out to fifty two and a half, and that's only right if the truck spent equal time at each. Usually it didn't, and the averaged number can be sitting right there among the five choices.`],
        terms: [[`Piecewise`,`Worked one rate at a time, then added up.`]],
        exam: `If a choice is the average of two rates from the problem, check whether the times were equal before you take it.` },
      { h: `The water plant's version`,
        body: [`Kit's plant runs a rate problem every shift. Pounds of chlorine a day equals the dose in milligrams per liter, times the flow in million gallons a day, times 8.34. The 8.34 is what a gallon of water weighs in pounds, and it turns a strength and a flow into a weight you could set on a scale.`,
          `You won't see that formula on the test. You'll see its shape a dozen times: a rate, times an amount, times one number that makes the units come out. The wrong choices tend to be the right formula with one piece left off, or with a decimal slid over a place.`,
          `It runs backward, too. Know the pounds and the flow and you can divide to get the dose. A formula with three parts in it is three different problems, depending on which part they hide from you.`],
        terms: [[`Pounds formula`,`mg/L times MGD times 8.34 gives pounds a day. Used here as practice for the shape, not as a test fact.`]],
        exam: `If a choice is yours times ten or a hundred, check your decimal before you check anything else.` },
      { h: `Read the question twice`,
        body: [`Plenty of wrong choices on this subtest are real numbers from the problem, answering a question nobody asked. The drops counted, not the rate. What's left after three loads, not how far short the plan was. Before you pick, read the last sentence again and say back what it wants.`,
          `Round first, and see where the answer has to land. 1.8 times 2.1 times 8.34 is about 4 times 8, so somewhere near 32. If you get 320 or 3.2, you don't need to check the arithmetic to know.`,
          `Scores are built from the number you get right, with no penalty for a wrong one, so a guess costs you nothing. If a problem is eating your minute, take your best estimate and move on.`],
        terms: [[`Estimate`,`A rounded answer worked first, so you know which choices are even possible.`]],
        exam: `Estimate before you calculate. A rough answer often rules out two or three of the five.` },
      { h: `The Self-Description Inventory`,
        body: [`After the break comes the Self-Description Inventory, 240 statements in 45 minutes on the current Pearson page. Each one says something about how you work or deal with people, and you mark how well it fits you on a five point scale: strongly disagree, moderately disagree, neither agree nor disagree, moderately agree, strongly agree.`,
          `It isn't graded, and in the 2015 pamphlet it feeds no composite. The pamphlet says it's there to match you with Air Force work that people who answer like you find satisfying, so a faked answer only points you at the wrong job. There are no right answers, and nobody can coach you to one. Answer as yourself, as you are now, not as the officer you think they're hoping for. Go fast, about eleven seconds a statement, and take the first honest answer. Answer the way you would on any other day of the week.`,
          `Pearson won't release your scores until every item has an answer, so don't leave any blank. And don't spend the forty five minutes trying to work out what each line is for. That's about the only way to do this one badly.`],
        terms: [[`Self-Description Inventory`,`The inventory that comes right after the break and isn't graded: 240 statements about you, no right answers.`]],
        exam: `On the inventory, take your first honest answer and keep moving. Thinking longer doesn't make an answer truer.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f5s1`, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`PS`, title:`Stroking dry`, q:`The pump is stroking and the line isn't moving. What's wrong with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, March 8, twenty to three in the morning, and the residual alarm went off. At the Tolliver plant that's a two note beep and a yellow box on the screen that says LOW RESIDUAL CLEARWELL. No horn. Nobody'd hear it from the lot, and at that hour nobody's in the lot. Yates, the night lead, was down at the river intake with a flashlight, cussing at the screen down there for packing up with leaves again. He did that most nights in March.`,`Point nine nine leaving the plant. They liked it around one four, and the alarm was set at one even. She got up, and the sheet in her coat crackled, and she let it.`,`Chemical room, end of the hall. The hypo pump's a gray plastic box on the wall about the size of a lunch pail, over a drum of the stuff. Hypochlorite. Call it bleach if you want, it's just a lot meaner than what's under your sink. The pump was clicking along like it was any other night. Click, green light, click, green light. The clear tube from the drum up to it was full of little bubbles, though, all the way up. And the line out the other side, the one to the injection point, wasn't going anywhere.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The bleach got cold in the night and went too thick to pull.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Her hand on the drum says room temperature, same as every night since October. Nothing in that room's ever been cold enough to thicken anything. And the bubbles are still sitting there.`,
          why:`I've seen hydraulic fluid go thick as syrup on a ramp in January, so I don't blame you for thinking it. In a heated room, though? And thick doesn't make bubbles. Look at what's in front of you first.`},
        {t:`Gas in the pump head, squeezing instead of pushing the liquid.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Bucket from under the sink. She held it under the priming valve on the pump head and cracked the valve a quarter turn, and it spat at her, foam and a burp of gas and then yellow. The clicking got heavy. You could hear it. She shut the valve. Boyd always said don't walk off from a thing the second it starts working, so she gave it another minute, standing there at three in the morning with a bucket of bleach foam, till the line was yellow all the way to the wall.`,
          why:`I learned this one in Guam, standing behind a crew chief who was bleeding a brake line at three in the morning. He called every bubble by name, and none of the names would go in a log. "Air squashes," he told me. "Fluid don't. That's the whole lesson." Your pump's got the same disease. Bleach is always coming apart a little and gassing off, the gas rides up into the pump head, and then the pump's squeezing a bubble all night and calling it work.`},
        {t:`The drum ran dry, and the pump's been pulling on air.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Air, sure. But the sight tube on the drum reads better than half, and day shift swapped that drum in on Sunday. So it wasn't the drum.`,
          why:`Half right. I'll give you the gas. A drum with that much left in it doesn't feed a pump air, though, so the gas never came from the bottom. It came out of the bleach. Fidgety stuff, bleach.`},
        {t:`Something's clogged downstream, and the stroke can't push past it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She pinches the outlet line. It gives like a garden hose with the water off. If something downstream were plugged, that line would be hard as a broom handle.`,
          why:`A clog pushes back. You'd feel it. Soft line, bubbles, a pump clicking along happy as a clam, that's gas, every time I ever saw it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s2`, domain:`Speed and Spatial`, task:`TR`, title:`The last good number`, q:`What does the residual row read at 0130?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Back at the console she pulled up the night's readings, every half hour since midnight, the way the plant printed them for the log. What she was after was the last good number before the drop. That'd tell her how long the town's water had gone out light.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Overnight readings, clearwell" say="Readings every half hour from midnight to three. Plant flow, in million gallons a day: one point two six, one point two five, one point two five, one point two four, one point two five, one point two five, one point two six. Chlorine residual, in milligrams per liter: one point four two, one point four one, one point four oh, one point three eight, one point two one, one point oh six, point nine four. Feed pump, percent: forty eight at every reading.">TIME · 0000 · 0030 · 0100 · 0130 · 0200 · 0230 · 0300<br>FLOW, MGD · 1.26 · 1.25 · 1.25 · 1.24 · 1.25 · 1.25 · 1.26<br>RESIDUAL, MG/L · 1.42 · 1.41 · 1.40 · 1.38 · 1.21 · 1.06 · 0.94<br>FEED PUMP, % · 48 · 48 · 48 · 48 · 48 · 48 · 48</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Read 1.21, the residual row under 0200.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Right row. One column too far. 1.21 is half an hour after it started falling, and it would have told her the pump quit later than it did.`,
          why:`Right row, wrong column, and some of the choices on the test sit exactly there. Count the columns from the header, not from the edge of the grid.`},
        {t:`Read 1.40, the residual row under 0100.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`That's 0100, one column early. It's a good number, but it isn't the last good one, and she'd be guessing about the half hour after it.`,
          why:`One column short. The times run across the top. Find the column first, then slide down to the row, and keep a finger on both till they meet.`},
        {t:`Read 1.24, the row above it under 0130.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Right column, wrong row. 1.24 is the flow, a million and a quarter gallons a day, and it has nothing to say about chlorine.`,
          why:`Your finger slid up a row on the way across. The labels are down the left side for a reason. Read the row name out loud if you have to.`},
        {t:`Read 1.38, the residual row under 0130.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Finger on 0130, finger on the residual row: 1.38. Then the neighbors, so she'd know what a slip would have looked like. 1.40 before it, 1.21 after, and 1.24 up in the flow row. So the pump had quit a little after half past one, and the water had gone out light for the better part of two hours. The bottom row said 48 percent all night. The pump had reported what it was told to do, not what it did.`,
          why:`Row, then column, then a look around the cell. On the test many of the wrong choices sit right next to the answer. And notice that bottom row. The pump said forty eight all night long. A gauge that shows you the setting isn't showing you the flow. I've been lied to by better instruments than that one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s3`, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`Pounds a day`, q:`What does the formula give for the night's line in the log?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The log had a line for chlorine fed, in pounds a day, and the night's was hers to fill in. March's dose was 2.4 milligrams per liter; Gene had set it on the first. The plant was making 1.25 million gallons a day.`,`Everybody at the plant knew the pounds formula the way they knew the gate code. Milligrams per liter, times million gallons a day, times 8.34, which is what a gallon of water weighs. Every line above hers in the log said 25.0, the same number night after night in four different handwritings. She worked it anyway, on the back of the clipboard. A number somebody else wrote down was somebody else's arithmetic.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`25.02 pounds a day: 3 times 8.34.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`2.4 times 1.25 is 3, the friendly kind of number. Three times 8.34 is 25.02. She wrote 25.0 on the line like everybody else. Then in the remarks she started on the rest: what the pump had actually done between half past one and a quarter after three, which wasn't 25 of anything.`,
          why:`Strength, times flow, times what a gallon weighs. The 8.34 is what turns milligrams per liter and millions of gallons into pounds you could lift. Every wrong choice here is that formula with one piece dropped or a decimal slid, which is how a lot of wrong choices get built. Round it first. Two and a half times one and a quarter is about three, three eights is twenty four, so the answer lives near twenty five.`},
        {t:`3 pounds a day: 2.4 times 1.25, and that's all.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She writes 3 and looks at it. Three pounds into a million and a quarter gallons wouldn't reach the first house. She'd left the 8.34 lying on the table.`,
          why:`Two thirds of a formula. You had the dose and the flow right and never turned them into pounds. The last number in a formula is usually the one that fixes the units. Leave it off and the answer comes out in nothing at all.`},
        {t:`20.02 pounds a day: 2.4 times 8.34, flow left out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`About twenty, close enough to look right in a hurry. Then she sees she never touched the flow. That's the dose, weighed, for one million gallons, and the plant was making a quarter more than that.`,
          why:`Close enough to fool you, which is the trouble with it. You weighed the dose and forgot how much water it went into. Every part of a formula is there for a reason. Count them off before you trust the number.`},
        {t:`250.2 pounds a day: 2.4 times 1.25 times 83.4.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Two hundred fifty pounds a day would empty a drum before lunch. She'd slid a decimal somewhere. It's 8.34, not 83.4.`,
          why:`Ten times too big. When an answer is yours times ten, look at the decimal before anything else. A quick rough answer would have caught it before you ever wrote it down.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s4`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The book`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Yates came back up from the intake at half past three with river mud on his boots and wet leaves stuck to the knees of his coveralls. He was sixty one and had worked nights at the plant since before Kit could drive. He read the screen over her shoulder, then the remarks she'd started, and pulled out the other chair.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Leave that out."</span> She asked him why. <span class="said" data-who="man">"It came back. Never got anywhere near the floor."</span> He rubbed his eyes with the heels of his hands. <span class="said" data-who="man">"And it's the third time this month. I primed it twice on my nights and kept it out of the book. You write tonight's, and Gene reads it at the huddle and asks why it's the first one."</span>`,`He didn't say it mean. He said it like a man reading a gauge he didn't care for, and then he sat and waited to see what she'd do with the pen.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave tonight out, and go check the pump herself every hour till seven.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She caps the pen. The remarks box stays empty. She walks down to the chemical room every hour until seven, and the pump behaves, and the log says 25.0 the way it always does. Nobody orders a thing.`,
          why:`That's covering. You fixed the pump and left the record wrong, and now you're the only check standing between that bubble and the town's water. That's a lot of weight to hang on a habit nobody wrote down. What happens the night you're off?`},
        {t:`Call Gene at home now and tell him about all three nights.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Gene answers on the sixth ring. She tells him the whole thing, Yates's two nights and all, while Yates stands in the doorway and listens to his own name go by. Gene says he'll be in early. Yates goes back down to the intake and stays there till the end of the shift.`,
          why:`Gene did need to know. You just got there over his head, at half past three, with his name in your mouth, and he'd have known at seven from an honest log. Those other two nights were Yates's to write, and you told them for him before he had the chance.`},
        {t:`Tell Yates she's logging tonight, and his two nights go in by seven.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I'm writing tonight's, Leon. All of it."</span> He looked at the pen a while. She told him the other two nights weren't hers. She hadn't seen them, and she wasn't going to write them for him. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"They go in the book before Gene reads it at seven. If they're not there, I'll tell him I knew."</span> Yates said she was a pain in the neck, and there wasn't much behind it. At twenty to seven he sat down at the far end of the desk and wrote two short lines under hers, with dates, and went home without saying good night, which for Yates was about normal.`,
          why:`Integrity, with a lead who runs that desk. Your own record you write true, whoever asks you not to. His nights are his to write, and you tell him so to his face with a time on it, before you'd carry them anywhere else. You didn't go over his head at half past three, and you didn't sign up to keep it for him. What would that book have said the night nobody caught it?`},
        {t:`Write tonight's entry true, and not say a word to Yates about it.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She writes it and doesn't say anything. At the huddle Gene reads her line out loud, turns around and asks Yates whether this has happened before. Yates says twice, in front of the whole day shift, and he looks straight at her the whole time he says it.`,
          why:`The record's right. The person isn't. He told you to your face what he wanted, and you answered him through the log, at a huddle, in front of a room. Same words, wrong place. You owed him the sentence before Gene got it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s5`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The yellow table`, q:`The sheet is on the table between them. How should Kit start?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`On Tuesday she slept till two, and at seven she was on Cedar Street at the brick ranch with the dead hedge. The porch light was on. Down the hall the television was going, low, the way it always was. On the drive over she'd thought about Yates in the chair beside hers at half past three, asking her to leave one night out of the book. She'd been leaving one out for seven years.`,`Vern had the reading book open to where they'd stopped and the egg timer beside it. She didn't sit. She took the sheet out of her coat and unfolded it on the yellow table and pressed the creases flat with the side of her hand, and he let her do all of that without a word.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Score sheet, October 2019" say="Air Force Officer Qualifying Test. Test date, nineteen October, twenty nineteen. Pilot, nineteen. CSO, forty four. Verbal, seventy one. Quantitative, thirty eight. Academic Aptitude, fifty five.">AFOQT · TEST DATE 19 OCT 2019<br>PILOT 19<br>CSO 44<br>VERBAL 71<br>QUANTITATIVE 38<br>ACADEMIC APTITUDE 55</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say the number first, then the story she told everybody about it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Pilot came back nineteen. You need twenty five."</span> She said the rest standing up, with her coat still on. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Boyd asked me to stay for the inspection that winter. He really asked. I said yes because yes was easier than saying nineteen out loud."</span> She kept her eyes on the sheet. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Then I let the shop have it. My mother. Graham. Boyd worst of all. He's spent seven years thinking he cost me something, and I let him."</span> Vern read the sheet top to bottom, every line. Then he put one finger under PILOT. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Instrument Comprehension?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Ran out of time. I guessed the last nine."</span> He nodded like that was a number he could use, and pointed at the other chair, and she sat.`,
          why:`Integrity, the hard kind, with nobody making you. The fact first, then your own part, and nobody else's name standing in front of it. Most people open with the reason, and the reason's the part I'd have listened to least. On the test too, the option that owns the miss before it explains it is usually the one they're after.`},
        {t:`Tell him Boyd asked her to stay that winter, then give him the number.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She starts with Boyd. Vern listens to the whole inspection, the spring, the shop. Then he looks down at the page, at the number she hasn't said yet, and waits. She has to say it anyway, and now it comes out sounding like the end of an excuse.`,
          why:`You put Boyd in front of it again. Seven years you've been leading with him. The reason went first, so the number sounded like an afterthought, and it was the one thing on that page that was yours.`},
        {t:`Slide it across, let him read it, and wait for him to ask.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She slides it over. He reads it. He doesn't ask. He guessed on Sunday, and he isn't going to start asking now, and she's sitting there making him. After a long minute she says it herself, which is where she'd have started if she'd just started.`,
          why:`Honest enough, and you made him do the work. A sheet on a table is a fact without its owner. He'd already guessed. What he hadn't heard yet was you saying it.`},
        {t:`Tell him the old scores stand anyway, so none of it matters much now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Old scores stand anyway. So."</span> Vern looks at her over the top of the page. That's the rule, he tells her. It isn't what she came here to say. She knows it isn't.`,
          why:`True, and beside the point. Superscoring is why this sheet can't hurt your next try. It isn't why you kept it under a tray of foam for seven years. Don't hide the thing behind the rule about the thing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s6`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`WK`, title:`One line`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He didn't say anything about the sheet. He got up and poured two coffees from the percolator, though she hadn't asked for one, and sat back down and turned his class ring on his finger, the one worn thin on one side. She'd seen him do it at the diner without knowing she'd noticed.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Spring of seventy three. You know about the gym. I bought this ring the winter before, and wore it to the flight physical so I'd have something to turn."</span> He turned it. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Eye chart on the back of a door. I read it right down to the line that counted, and that line came out like somebody'd breathed on it. One line. The flight surgeon wrote one word on my form, and that night I went and looked it up, like the dictionary might've had it wrong."</span>`,`He took a pencil out of the coffee can and printed it in the margin of her sheet, under her 19, in his capitals. ACUITY. It was the first word anybody had ever written on that page. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Closest in meaning, Sergeant."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him FIRMNESS.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Firmness."</span> He shakes his head, not unkindly. Firm is a handshake, he says. The chart never asked for his grip.`,
          why:`Firm is about holding, and acuity is about edge. When a choice is just a good thing you'd want in a pilot, hold it up against the word, not the job.`},
        {t:`Tell him SOURNESS.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Sourness."</span> He almost smiles. That's what acid means, he says. Not this word.`,
          why:`Acid and acuity are distant cousins, both from an old word for sharp, but acid went to sour and acuity stayed sharp. Pick the meaning of the word in front of you, not its family.`},
        {t:`Tell him STEADINESS.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Steadiness."</span> He thinks about it. His hands were steady, he says. That wasn't the line.`,
          why:`Close, and I'd have liked it on my form. But a steady eye isn't a sharp one. A word for something nearby and good isn't the word. Closest in meaning means the meaning, not the neighborhood.`},
        {t:`Tell him SHARPNESS.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Sharpness."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Sharpness. I had all of it but one line."</span> The navigator school had offered him a seat the same week, he said, and he'd said yes before the man finished asking. He left the word where it was, in the margin under her 19, and she let it stay.`,
          why:`A, C, U is sharp. Acute, acumen, the needle in acupuncture. Acuity is sharpness, mostly of the eye or the mind. When a choice only sounds like the word, or only sits near it, ask which word is actually in front of you. Mine was acuity. I've spelled it right ever since.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s7`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`Not the question`, q:`How much faster was the leak going at the second count?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"So I navigated. Tolliver's tankers till they pulled the seat, and from eighty five on, the rest of the month, I dispatched for a freight outfit in Kansas City."</span> He drank some coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Did my dispatcher course in eighty five with a kid from Denver named Walt Herrera, twenty three, best weather head in the room. Thirty one years after that I signed releases for airplanes I never got on. My name went next to the captain's on every one of them."</span>`,`Kit had the card in her coat too. She'd written it Monday at four in the morning, after the pump came back, in pen on an index card out of the supply drawer at the plant. She took it out and set it face up by his coffee. I WANT TO FLY. It was the sentence from LETTER 2, the one she'd never printed.`,`Vern read it once. He stood it up against the percolator, where it would be in front of him every time he poured, and went down the hall to the den. He came back with a log out of the box.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Arithmetic."</span>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Navigator's log, February 2001" by="Vern" say="My log, twenty seven February, oh one. Tolliver, preflight. Crew chief found fuel weeping at a panel under the left wing. Oh five twenty, counted twelve drops in four minutes. Oh five fifty, counted fifteen drops in three minutes. Crew chief wrote it up. No go. I had the fuel to fly it. Not the question.">27 FEB 01. TOLLIVER. PREFLIGHT.<br>CREW CHIEF FOUND FUEL WEEPING AT A PANEL, LEFT WING.<br>0520 COUNTED 12 DROPS IN 4 MIN.<br>0550 COUNTED 15 DROPS IN 3 MIN.<br>CREW CHIEF WROTE IT UP. NO GO.<br>I HAD THE FUEL TO FLY IT. NOT THE QUESTION.</prop>`,`She didn't ask whose jet it was.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Three drops a minute faster: fifteen against twelve.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fifteen less twelve is three. Then she looks at the minutes next to them. The two counts didn't run the same length of time, so the raw numbers don't line up against each other.`,
          why:`You compared the counts and skipped the clocks. Twelve took four minutes and fifteen took three. Turn both into per minute first, then subtract.`},
        {t:`Two drops a minute faster: five a minute against three.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Twelve in four minutes is three a minute. Fifteen in three is five. Two a minute faster in half an hour, and still climbing when they quit counting. She looked at his last line a long time. I had the fuel to fly it. Not the question. For seven years she'd let a nineteen answer whether she should fly. Nobody had asked her whether she wanted to. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Crew chief was right,"</span> Vern said, and that was all he said about that.`,
          why:`Put both counts on the same clock before you compare them. Per minute, per hour, per anything, as long as it's the same per. Fifteen looks like a little more than twelve until you notice it got there a minute quicker. And here's what that crew chief taught me. I could work out what the leak would cost us in fuel, to the pound. That arithmetic answered a question nobody'd asked.`},
        {t:`Faster, but she'd want a third count before she said how much.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She could take a third count, sure, if she were standing under that wing. She doesn't need one to say how much faster the first two were. The numbers are right there on his page.`,
          why:`Good instinct. A crew chief wants the trend, not one snapshot. But the question asked how much faster, and two counts answer it. Don't go looking for more data than the question wants.`},
        {t:`A quarter faster: fifteen is a quarter more than twelve.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fifteen is a quarter more than twelve. It got there a minute quicker, though, so a quarter's too small. Per minute it's five against three.`,
          why:`You took a percent of the raw counts, and they weren't counted on the same clock, so the percent's no good. Five a minute against three is two more a minute. That's two thirds faster, not a quarter.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s8`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The coat on the hook`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a4=((all||{})[4]||{}).picks||{};const told=a4[7]===undefined||a4[7]===1;return [`Graham was at the kitchen table on Orchard Street with a stack of lab reports and a red pen when she got home at half past nine. He'd left the porch light on and the radio off. Behind him on the fridge he'd boxed off Saturday, April 3, in green: PRACTICE TEST, 5 HRS, ME ON THE CLOCK.`,told?`On Saturday she'd told him all of the wheel, her own part in it too, standing at that counter. That had been hard. This one she'd been carrying a lot longer.`:a4[7]===2?`On Saturday she'd given him a version of the wheel, a good one with her part trimmed off. She knew the shape of that habit. This was the oldest one she had.`:`On Saturday she'd given him the jet going late and not much else. She knew the shape of that habit. This was the oldest one she had.`,`She hung her coat on the hook by the door with the sheet still in the inside pocket. He looked up from a sophomore's graph and took her in, the coat and the hook and her face. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"You look like he made you do long division for two hours straight. How was Vern tonight?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him Vern was fine, and leave the coat where it hangs.`, s:0, flag:`toldnoone`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Fine. Long."</span> He nods and goes back to his graphs. The coat stays on the hook with the sheet in it, about six feet from his chair, all evening.`,
          why:`A closed door with a coat hung on it. The longer a thing like that hangs there, the likelier it is somebody else opens it. On the test and at home, keeping the people closest to you in the dark lands near the bottom.`},
        {t:`Tell him Vern guessed she'd sat the test before, and leave it there.`, s:1, flag:`toldnoone`, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"He guessed I'd sat it before."</span> Graham shrugs. She did, in 2019. He knows that part. He's always known that part. It's the rest he doesn't have, and she gives him none of it.`,
          why:`True, and empty. You told him something he already knew so it would feel like telling. That's a report shaped to make you feel honest. Who was it for?`},
        {t:`Tell him a part of 2019 is hers, and give him a day.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"There's a part of 2019 you haven't heard. It's mine. It isn't Boyd's."</span> Graham put the red pen down on the graph. <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Do you want to tell me now?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Not tonight. Tonight it'd come out as Boyd's story with a different ending. Saturday the third, after the practice test. All of it, with the paper on the table."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Graham">"Okay."</span> It came right away this time, no beat in front of it. Then she heard what she'd said. After the practice test. Nine years of afters, and she'd just handed him one more, and he'd taken it the way he took all the others. Before he went up to bed he took the marker off the fridge and wrote 2019 in the square for Saturday, April 3, small, in his own black.`,
          why:`Communication. You didn't hand him all of it, and you didn't let him think there was nothing to hand. He knows it's there, he knows whose it is, and he knows when he gets it. In my trade a release has a time on it, and nobody slides it. You named the time. Now you owe it.`},
        {t:`Tell him it's the same old Boyd story, and ask about his week.`, s:0, flag:`toldnoone`, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Same old story. Boyd and the inspection."</span> He nods. He's heard it so many times he doesn't hear it anymore. She asks about the sophomores and he tells her, and she listens to every word, and none of it lands.`,
          why:`You told the old version on purpose to the one person who most deserved the new one. That isn't keeping a secret anymore. It's telling the lie again, and every retelling makes it harder to take back.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s9`, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`The ten gallon line`, q:`When will the drum be down to its ten gallon line?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const t=(s.picks||{})[3];return [t===0?`It lost prime again Wednesday night, on Yates's shift. He primed it, and neither of them wrote it down, and nobody ordered anything, because nobody but the two of them knew there was anything to order.`:t===1?`Gene had come in at a quarter after four Monday morning with his hair still wet, and by the huddle he'd ordered a valve. Yates hadn't said ten words to her since.`:t===3?`At Monday's huddle Gene had read her line out loud and then asked Yates, in front of the day shift, whether it had happened before. He ordered a valve before lunch. Yates hadn't said ten words to her since.`:`At Monday's huddle Gene read all three lines, hers and Yates's two under it, and didn't make a speech about any of them. He ordered a valve before lunch.`,t===0?`Her next stretch of nights had started at eleven that night too. At three Thursday morning she walked down and looked at the pump head without anybody asking her to.`:`Her next stretch of nights started at eleven Wednesday night. There was a note taped to the pump in Gene's square printing: DEGAS VALVE ORDERED. CHECK THE HEAD EVERY HOUR TILL IT COMES. At three Thursday morning she did the check.`,`Then she read the drum. At eleven, when she came on, the sight tube had said 50 gallons. Now it said 46. The plant changed drums at 10, never lower, because the bottom of a drum was where the worst of it settled. Day shift would want to know when to have the next one on the dolly.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Saturday at 1 AM: all forty six gallons at a gallon an hour.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Forty six hours from three Thursday morning runs out to one in the morning Saturday. Then she remembers the ten gallons that never leave the drum. Those were never hers to spend.`,
          why:`You found the rate fine and then counted gallons you can't use. Take the floor off before you divide. What's in the drum and what you're allowed to pull aren't the same number.`},
        {t:`Thursday at 1 PM: the ten gallons at a gallon an hour.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Ten gallons at a gallon an hour is ten hours. That's how long the floor would last on its own, which isn't anything she was asked.`,
          why:`You worked the floor instead of what's sitting above it. The question wanted the gallons above the line.`},
        {t:`Friday at 11 AM: thirty six hours from eleven Wednesday night.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The arithmetic's right and the clock's wrong. She counted from the first reading, and four of those gallons are already gone. Day shift would have pulled a drum with four good gallons above the line.`,
          why:`You did the hard part and started the clock in the wrong place. Thirty six gallons are left above the line now, at three, not at eleven. Count from the reading you're standing at.`},
        {t:`Friday at 3 PM: thirty six gallons at a gallon an hour.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Fifty to forty six in four hours is a gallon an hour, twenty four a day, same as the log said. Forty six less the ten she couldn't use is thirty six. Thirty six gallons at one an hour is thirty six hours, and three Thursday morning plus thirty six hours is three Friday afternoon. She wrote CHANGE DRUM FRI 1500 on the board by the door and put her initials after it.`,
          why:`Three steps, and each one feeds the next. Get the rate from the two readings, take off what you can't use, then divide and add it to the clock from where you're standing. Four gallons in four hours is one an hour. Thirty six above the line is thirty six hours. The step people skip is the floor, the ten gallons nobody's allowed to pull.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s10`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SDI`, title:`Number eleven`, q:`It's number eleven, and the egg timer's running. How should she answer it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The next Tuesday the card was still standing against the percolator.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"This one I can't teach you."</span> He laid a typed page on the table. Twenty statements, each with a row of five boxes, strongly disagree on one end and strongly agree on the other. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"None of these came off the test. I wrote them at this table Sunday. The real one's two hundred forty in forty five minutes, and nobody grades it, and nobody can tell you what to put. Eleven seconds a line."</span> He cranked the egg timer to four.`,`She went fast. I like to know the plan before I start. Moderately agree. I'd rather fix a thing myself than ask for help. Strongly agree. I stay calm when things go wrong. Moderately agree. Then number eleven. When I want something, I say so.`,`Her pencil was in the moderately agree box before she'd read to the end, the way your hand finds the light switch in your own kitchen. Then it stopped there.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Mark what a good officer candidate would say, and keep going.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`She leaves it on moderately agree. An officer says what she wants, surely. Then twelve, then thirteen, and she's answering for some woman she's never met, and it's taking her twice as long.`,
          why:`That's faking, and it's slow. You'd be answering two hundred forty lines for somebody else at eleven seconds apiece. Nothing on it gets graded. Answer for you.`},
        {t:`Mark what's true of her today, the first honest answer, and go on.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:2},
          after:`She moved the pencil two boxes over and marked moderately disagree. It took about two seconds. For seven years she'd described herself as the crew chief who stayed, and she'd described her well, and it had been the wrong woman. She did the last nine fast. When the timer rang, Vern turned the page face down without reading a word of it. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Those are yours."</span>`,
          why:`Nothing to get right on that one. It isn't graded, and in the 2015 pamphlet it feeds no composite. Answer as you are today, quick, and the same way you'd answer it on any other day. The people who do it badly are the ones trying to work out what each line is for. Guessing what they want makes you slower, and it makes you somebody you aren't.`},
        {t:`Stop and think it through until she's sure she has it right.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`She thinks about it. Does she? She said it once, to Graham, in January. Does once count? The egg timer rings with nine statements still blank.`,
          why:`Thinking longer doesn't make it truer. Eleven seconds a line is the pace, and your first honest answer is almost always the one you'd have landed on anyway. Mark it and go.`},
        {t:`Skip it, and come back to it if there's time at the end.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`She skips it. When the timer rings, the empty row is the only thing on the page she can see.`,
          why:`Pearson won't release your scores until every item has an answer, so a skip is just a later answer, given tired. And there's nothing on this one to come back for.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s11`, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`On the boom`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`After that he set the night's arithmetic, ten problems in his capitals, and she did them on the egg timer.`,`A receiver came onto the boom needing 21,000 pounds. For the first six minutes the boom passed fuel at 2,500 pounds a minute. Then the receiver's tanks started to fill, the flow slowed to 1,500 pounds a minute, and it stayed there to the end.`,`Underneath, in his capitals: HOW LONG ON THE BOOM. He'd underlined LONG.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ten minutes: six fast, then four slow.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`Six minutes at twenty five hundred is fifteen thousand. That leaves six thousand at fifteen hundred a minute, four minutes more. Ten. She wrote it and circled it. Vern looked at the circle, then up at the stove clock. Nine already. He said that would do for one night.`,
          why:`When the rate changes partway, cut the problem where it changes and work each piece. Averaging two rates only works when each one ran the same length of time, and these didn't. Six minutes fast, four slow. Every boom operator I ever flew with said the last few thousand pounds take the longest, and none of them was ever wrong about it.`},
        {t:`Ten and a half minutes, at the average of the two rates.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Two thousand a minute, ten and a half minutes. It's close enough to feel right. But the fast rate ran six minutes and the slow one four, so they don't get an equal say.`,
          why:`Close, and wrong in a way that's easy to fall for. You can only average two rates that ran for the same time, and these didn't. Work the pieces.`},
        {t:`Eight minutes twenty four seconds, all of it at the fast rate.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Twenty one thousand over twenty five hundred is eight point four, eight minutes and twenty four seconds. Except the flow didn't stay at twenty five hundred.`,
          why:`You used the fast rate all the way. The problem told you it slowed. Read for the word then.`},
        {t:`Fourteen minutes, all of it at the slow rate.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fourteen minutes, if the boom had dragged along at fifteen hundred the whole way. It didn't. The first six ran fast.`,
          why:`Slow rate start to finish, and the first six minutes weren't slow. Cut it into pieces.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s12`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Gooseberry`, q:`What should she ask Vern to keep?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const tn=(s.flags||{}).toldnoone||(G||{}).toldnoone;return [`At the front door that night the television down the hall went quiet, and a small woman came out of there. Short white hair, reading glasses shoved up on her head, a pie in a glass dish under a dish towel.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Patrice">"You're the sergeant. I'm Patrice. It's gooseberry. I froze them in June, and he'll eat the whole thing standing up if it stays in this house."</span> She put it in Kit's hands. Then she looked back toward the kitchen. Vern had the water running over the cups and was pretending he couldn't hear. <span class="said" data-who="Patrice">"He hasn't talked this much in years. He won't tell me what about, which is how I know it's yours."</span> She tipped her head at the percolator. <span class="said" data-who="Patrice">"And that card's been against my coffee pot a week. He moves it to pour and puts it back."</span>`,`On Saturday, March 20, Kit brought the dish back washed, to the heritage room, because she knew he'd be there. Nobody had wandered in. He had the reading book open on the cleared table, and he shoved the egg timer over so the dish had somewhere to go.`,tn?`Graham still had the Boyd version. The sheet was still in her coat on the hook by the door, and in nine years he'd never once gone through her coat.`:`Graham had a date. It was on the whiteboard in his black marker, 2019 in the square for April 3, and she saw it every morning when she got the milk out.`,`She hadn't come about the dish.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask him to keep 2019 between the two of them, for good.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Between us. For good."</span> Vern doesn't answer right away. He looks at her the way he looks at a log entry with no time on it.`,
          why:`You asked him to help you keep a lie running. He'll keep your confidence. That's different from keeping it for good, and you knew that when you asked.`},
        {t:`Ask him to keep it until after the test in May.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Till after the test."</span> He says the word back to her, after, quietly, like he's reading it off a page she wrote.`,
          why:`Right idea, wrong clock. You'd be putting the truth behind a score again, the same as 2019, only the other way around. If it waits on the number, the number decides when you're honest.`},
        {t:`Ask him to keep it till she's told Graham and Boyd herself.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Keep it till I've told Graham and Boyd myself. Graham first. Then ask me if I did."</span> Vern let her get all the way to the end of it. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"I can ask."</span>`,
          why:`Integrity, with some Communication in it. You didn't ask him to carry it for you or to bury it. You asked him to hold it while you deliver it yourself, in order, to the people it belongs to, and you gave him leave to check on you. The test likes that shape too: you own the job, and you let somebody hold you to it.`},
        {t:`Ask him to tell the Lieutenant about 2019 before somebody else can.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Would you tell the Lieutenant? Before it gets to her some other way?"</span> Vern closes the reading book. He doesn't call colonels, he tells her. And he doesn't carry that for anybody.`,
          why:`You asked him to carry your news up the chain on his old history with her. That goes around every person it belongs to first, and it spends a name that isn't yours to spend. He'd have said no in 2001 too.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f5s7`, title:`Per what?`, intro:`The crew chief in Vern's log counted on two different clocks. Put each of these on one clock before you answer.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Which leak is faster: 9 drops in 3 minutes, or 20 drops in 5 minutes?`, choices:[`The first`,`The second`,`They're the same`], answer:1, why:`Three a minute against four. The second one's dripping faster, even though five minutes sounds slow.`},
      {prompt:`A pump uses 36 gallons a day. How many gallons is that an hour?`, choices:[`1.5`,`3`,`0.67`], answer:0, why:`36 over 24 hours is 1.5 an hour. Flip it over and you get 0.67, which is hours per gallon, the rate standing on its head.`},
      {prompt:`A truck goes 30 miles in 40 minutes. How fast is that in miles an hour?`, choices:[`75`,`1.33`,`45`], answer:2, why:`Forty minutes is two thirds of an hour, and 30 divided by two thirds is 45. If you got 75, I'd bet you read forty minutes as 0.4 hours.`},
      {prompt:`A jet burns 4,800 pounds in 40 minutes. What's that per hour?`, choices:[`6,000`,`7,200`,`120`], answer:1, why:`That's 120 pounds a minute, and an hour has sixty of those, so 7,200. The 120 is true. It's just per minute.`},
      {prompt:`A fill runs 3,000 gallons an hour for 2 hours, then 1,000 gallons an hour for 3 hours. How much in all?`, choices:[`9,000`,`10,000`,`8,000`], answer:0, why:`6,000 in the first piece, 3,000 in the second, 9,000 in all. Work each piece, then add.`},
      {prompt:`A dose of 2 mg/L goes into 0.5 million gallons a day. How many pounds a day is that?`, choices:[`1`,`16.68`,`8.34`], answer:2, why:`2 times 0.5 is 1, and 1 times 8.34 is 8.34. Plenty of people stop at the 1.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`A small plant treats 0.5 million gallons a day at a chlorine dose of 3.0 milligrams per liter. Pounds per day equals mg/L times MGD times 8.34. How many pounds of chlorine does it feed a day?`, opts:[`12.51`,`1.5`,`4.17`,`25.02`,`125.1`], a:0, why:`3.0 times 0.5 is 1.5, and 1.5 times 8.34 is 12.51. 1.5 leaves off the 8.34, 4.17 leaves off the dose, 25.02 doubles it, and 125.1 slides the decimal.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`A plant feeds 50.04 pounds of chlorine a day into a flow of 2.0 million gallons a day. Using pounds per day equals mg/L times MGD times 8.34, what is the dose in mg/L?`, opts:[`1.5`,`3.0`,`6.0`,`12.0`,`25.02`], a:1, why:`Run it backward: 50.04 divided by (2.0 times 8.34). 2.0 times 8.34 is 16.68, and 50.04 over 16.68 is 3.0. Dividing by 8.34 alone gives 6.0, and dividing by the flow alone gives 25.02.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`A storage tank holds 0.25 million gallons. The crew needs a chlorine dose of 50 mg/L through the whole tank. Using mg/L times million gallons times 8.34, how many pounds of chlorine is that?`, opts:[`12.5`,`52.13`,`208.5`,`1,042.5`,`104.25`], a:4, why:`The formula works for a volume as well as a daily flow. 50 times 0.25 is 12.5, and 12.5 times 8.34 is 104.25. 12.5 forgot the 8.34, and 1,042.5 slid the decimal.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`At 8:00 AM a water tank's gauge reads 640 gallons. At 11:00 AM it reads 520 gallons. If it keeps falling at the same rate, when will it be empty?`, opts:[`9:00 PM`,`11:00 PM`,`midnight`,`1:00 AM`,`3:00 AM`], a:2, why:`It lost 120 gallons in 3 hours, 40 an hour. The 520 left lasts 13 hours, and 11 AM plus 13 hours is midnight. 9 PM counts the 13 hours from 8 AM instead of from 11.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`A convoy drives 90 miles at 45 miles an hour, then 60 more miles at 60 miles an hour. How long does the whole trip take?`, opts:[`2 hours`,`2 hours 30 minutes`,`2 hours 50 minutes`,`3 hours`,`3 hours 20 minutes`], a:3, why:`Work each piece. 90 miles at 45 is 2 hours, and 60 at 60 is 1 more, so 3 hours. 2 hours 30 minutes runs all 150 miles at 60, 3 hours 20 minutes runs it all at 45, and 2 hours 50 minutes swaps the two speeds.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`A fitting drips 15 drops a minute, and 20 drops make 1 milliliter. How many milliliters does it lose in an hour?`, opts:[`45`,`0.75`,`300`,`900`,`18,000`], a:0, why:`15 drops a minute is 900 drops an hour, and 900 over 20 is 45 milliliters. 0.75 is the milliliters per minute, 900 is drops, not milliliters. 300 multiplies the per minute drops by 20, and 18,000 does it for the whole hour.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`The chlorine residual in a water line falls from 1.6 mg/L to 1.2 mg/L. By what percent did it fall?`, opts:[`0.4 percent`,`25 percent`,`33 1/3 percent`,`40 percent`,`75 percent`], a:1, why:`It fell 0.4. Percent change is the change over where it started: 0.4 over 1.6 is a quarter, 25 percent. 33 1/3 divides by the new value, and 75 percent is what's left, not what fell.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`Two wells feed a water plant in a 3 to 2 ratio, and the first well is putting in 0.9 million gallons a day. What are the two wells putting in together, in million gallons a day?`, opts:[`0.6`,`1.2`,`1.35`,`2.25`,`1.5`], a:4, why:`If three parts are 0.9, one part's 0.3, and both wells together are five parts: 1.5 million gallons a day. 0.6 is just the second well. 1.35 and 2.25 come from multiplying 0.9 by 1.5 and by 2.5.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`Out on the refueling track a tanker's holding 300 knots, and its receiver is coming up the same track from 60 nautical miles back at 420 knots. How long until the receiver catches up?`, opts:[`5 minutes`,`about 8.6 minutes`,`12 minutes`,`30 minutes`,`2 hours`], a:3, why:`I'd do this one on my fingers. The receiver gains 120 knots, and 120 knots is 2 miles a minute. Sixty miles at 2 a minute is half an hour. Adding the speeds, which gets you 5 minutes, is for two airplanes pointed at each other, and that's a different and much worse afternoon.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`On a long sortie a jet goes through 33,600 pounds of fuel in 4 hours 12 minutes. On average, how many pounds an hour is that?`, opts:[`6,720`,`7,000`,`8,000`,`8,155`,`8,400`], a:2, why:`The 12 minutes is where everybody trips. It's a fifth of an hour, so the time is 4.2 hours, and 33,600 over 4.2 is 8,000. Call it 4.12 and you'll get about 8,155. Drop the 12 minutes and you'll get 8,400.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`The plant's water truck runs a 42 mile sampling route, and the whole loop takes 56 minutes. What's its average speed, in miles an hour?`, opts:[`0.75`,`42`,`56`,`75`,`45`], a:4, why:`Fifty six minutes is 56 sixtieths of an hour. So it's 42 times 60, divided by 56, and that's 45. If you got 0.75 you found miles a minute, and if you got 75 you turned 56 minutes into 0.56 hours, a slip I've watched a lot of good people make.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`A fuel cell's losing 18 pounds every 45 minutes, steady as a clock. What's that per hour, in pounds?`, opts:[`24`,`0.4`,`13.5`,`27`,`810`], a:0, why:`Forty five minutes is three quarters of an hour, and 18 divided by three quarters is 24. If you got 13.5, you multiplied where you meant to divide. 0.4 is pounds a minute, which is true and isn't what they asked.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`An airplane climbs out of 2,000 feet at 1,500 feet a minute and levels off at 17,000. How many minutes did the climb take?`, opts:[`about 1.3 minutes`,`10 minutes`,`about 11.3 minutes`,`about 12.7 minutes`,`100 minutes`], a:1, why:`It only climbed 15,000 feet, since it started at 2,000. Fifteen thousand at 1,500 a minute is 10 minutes. About 11.3 forgot where it started from, and about 12.7 added the 2,000 on instead of taking it off.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`You're doing 420 knots over the ground. How many minutes will it take you to cover 98 nautical miles?`, opts:[`about 4.3 minutes`,`7 minutes`,`14 minutes`,`23 minutes`,`42 minutes`], a:2, why:`Four twenty knots is 7 nautical miles a minute, and 98 over 7 is 14. If you did 98 over 420 you got 0.233 hours, and 23 minutes is that decimal read as if it were minutes.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`In summer the plant's flow of 1.2 million gallons a day goes up 15 percent. What's the summer flow, in million gallons a day?`, opts:[`1.02`,`1.35`,`1.44`,`1.38`,`1.80`], a:3, why:`Fifteen percent of 1.2 is 0.18, so the summer flow is 1.38. If you got 1.35 you added 0.15 straight on, which is fifteen hundredths, not fifteen percent of anything. 1.02 went the wrong direction.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`The old pump empties a 12,000 gallon clearwell in 4 hours. The new one's 50 percent faster. How long does the new one take to empty it?`, opts:[`2 hours`,`3 hours`,`3 hours 20 minutes`,`6 hours`,`2 hours 40 minutes`], a:4, why:`The old one moves 3,000 gallons an hour, so the new one moves 4,500. Twelve thousand over 4,500 is 2 and 2/3 hours, which is 2 hours 40 minutes. Two hours would take a pump twice as fast, and Gene isn't buying one of those.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`There's a seep on the jet losing 0.3 pounds of fuel an hour. Over a 5 hour 20 minute sortie, how many pounds does it lose?`, opts:[`1.5`,`1.56`,`1.6`,`1.8`,`16`], a:2, why:`Twenty minutes is a third of an hour, so the sortie is 5 1/3 hours, and 0.3 times 5 1/3 is 1.6 pounds. 1.56 reads 5 hours 20 minutes as 5.2. 1.5 leaves the 20 minutes sitting on the ramp.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`Two pumps run at once. One moves 240 gallons a minute and the other 160. How long will they take to move 12,000 gallons together?`, opts:[`30 minutes`,`50 minutes`,`60 minutes`,`75 minutes`,`125 minutes`], a:0, why:`Together they move 400 gallons a minute, and 12,000 over 400 is 30 minutes. 60 minutes uses the average of the two pumps, which is one pump's worth, not two.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`A tanker has 40,000 pounds of fuel to offload. Its three receivers take 9,000, 11,500 and 13,500 pounds. What percent of the 40,000 did it offload?`, opts:[`15 percent`,`34 percent`,`75 percent`,`85 percent`,`about 118 percent`], a:3, why:`The three loads add to 34,000. 34,000 over 40,000 is 0.85, 85 percent. 15 percent is what was left, and about 118 percent divides the wrong way.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`},
    {q:`A plant uses 6.5 gallons of hypochlorite a day, and it costs $1.80 a gallon. What does a 31 day month of it cost?`, opts:[`$11.70`,`$362.70`,`$201.50`,`$351.00`,`$378.00`], a:1, why:`One day costs 6.5 times $1.80, which is $11.70. 31 days is $362.70. $351.00 is a 30 day month, and $201.50 is the gallons, 6.5 times 31, written as dollars.`, task:`AR`, domain:`Quantitative`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Vern looked at the pie dish for a while. Patrice's dish, washed, with the dish towel folded on top of it the way Patrice had sent it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"It isn't mine to tell, Sergeant."</span> He moved the dish an inch, for no reason. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"And it isn't yours to keep forever."</span>`,
    `Then he dug in the corduroy jacket, the one from the diner in January, and came up with a log folded once. He gave her logs on Tuesdays, at the end of the night, picked for the week's work. This wasn't a Tuesday, and he didn't say what it was for. He held it out across the table anyway.`,
    `Kit took it and didn't open it. It rode home on the passenger seat, under her right hand at every stop sign on the river road.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch05.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Wind Triangle`
});
